    "summary": "Intense second‑set jams – The Other One, Eyes Of The World, Playin’ In The Band",
    "blurb": "The first set rolls along solidly before exploding into a soaring second set where Truckin' segues into The Other One and then Eyes Of The World, climaxing with an expansive Playing In The Band. Jerry’s guitar is spacey and intense while Phil lays down animated bass lines. Recording flaws are present but the musical performance shines.",
    "review": "On March 15, 1973 the Dead took the stage at Nassau Coliseum for a show steeped in emotion – it was the first gig after Pig Pen’s death and the band arranged themselves in a circle, feeding off each other's energy. The first set offered familiar tunes like Box of Rain and Deal, but the real magic emerged in the second set. A gritty soundboard capture still manages to convey the intensity of the Truckin' → The Other One → Eyes Of The World sequence, with Jerry Garcia delivering several “meltdown” guitar moments that soar over Keith Godchaux’s spacious keys and Bill Kreutzmann’s driving drums. Phil Lesh’s bass is particularly animated throughout the jams, and the set closes on a triumphant Playing In The Band that feels both cathartic and celebratory despite occasional channel drops and splice artifacts. While the recording is far from pristine – mono sections, splices in Eyes Of The World and some vocal distortion linger – this performance remains one of the era’s most emotionally charged showcases.",
